  m_Name: tier2_advanced
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: WordRPG::WordRPG.Words.WordDatabase
  tier: 2
  regionId: advanced
  regionName: "\uC232\xB7\uC815\uAE00\xB7\uB3D9\uAD74"
  rewards:
  - regionId: forest
    regionName: "\uC232"
    keepsake: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 05119db81daf98c49945dbb01ce077ad, type: 2}
    gold: 700
    wordsNeeded: 100
    storyOrder: 3
  - regionId: jungle
    regionName: "\uBA54\uC544\uB9AC \uC815\uAE00"
    keepsake: {fileID: 11400000, guid: d7331af346374d9498d7f7ff5e31ae27, type: 2}
    gold: 800
    wordsNeeded: 200
    storyOrder: 4
  - regionId: cave
    regionName: "\uC5B4\uB450\uC6B4 \uBC00\uB9BC \uB3D9\uAD74"
    keepsake: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 528fd9a2b4a378f4eaf4f4d0509121e2, type: 2}
    gold: 900
    wordsNeeded: 0
    storyOrder: 5
  journal: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c8fe83c5587070442976072e0cbb9051, type: 2}
  words:
  - id: abuse
    english: abuse
    meaning: "\uB0A8\uC6A9, \uD559\uB300"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule says this trip is animal abuse. I agree.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC774 \uC5EC\uD589\uC774 \uB3D9\uBB3C \uD559\uB300\uB77C\uACE0
      \uD55C\uB2E4. \uB098\uB3C4 \uB3D9\uC758\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 85
    storyHeading: 
  - id: academy
    english: academy
    meaning: "\uD559\uC6D0, \uD559\uD68C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The academy taught me how to draw maps.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD559\uC6D0\uC740 \uB0B4\uAC8C \uC9C0\uB3C4 \uADF8\uB9AC\uB294
      \uBC95\uC744 \uAC00\uB974\uCCE4\uB2E4."
    story: 2
    storyHeading: 
  - id: accurate
    english: accurate
    meaning: "\uC815\uD655\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My map was accurate for about one hour.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uB294 \uD55C \uC2DC\uAC04\uCBE4 \uC815\uD655\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 20
    storyHeading: 
  - id: acid
    english: acid
    meaning: "\uC0B0(\u9178)"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Spider spit works like acid on paper.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC70\uBBF8 \uCE68\uC740 \uC885\uC774\uC5D0 \uC0B0\uCC98\uB7FC
      \uC791\uC6A9\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 272
    storyHeading: 
  - id: acquisition
    english: acquisition
    meaning: "\uC2B5\uB4DD, \uC778\uC218"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My latest acquisition is a spoon with no handle.
    exampleMeaning: "\uCD5C\uADFC \uC2B5\uB4DD\uD488\uC740 \uC190\uC7A1\uC774 \uC5C6\uB294
      \uC21F\uAC00\uB77D\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 249
    storyHeading: 
  - id: agency
    english: agency
    meaning: "\uB300\uB9AC\uC810, \uAE30\uAD00"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: No agency has ever come this far.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5B4\uB5A4 \uAE30\uAD00\uB3C4 \uC5EC\uAE30\uAE4C\uC9C0 \uC628
      \uC801 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 220
    storyHeading: 
  - id: agenda
    english: agenda
    meaning: "\uC548\uAC74, \uC758\uC81C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The first item on the agenda was lunch.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC548\uAC74\uC758 \uCCAB \uD56D\uBAA9\uC740 \uC810\uC2EC\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 9
    storyHeading: 
  - id: agriculture
    english: agriculture
    meaning: "\uB18D\uC5C5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The people here once lived by agriculture.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30 \uC0AC\uB78C\uB4E4\uC740 \uD55C\uB54C \uB18D\uC5C5\uC73C\uB85C
      \uC0B4\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 186
    storyHeading: 
  - id: alert
    english: alert
    meaning: "\uACBD\uACC4\uD558\uB294, \uAE30\uBBFC\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: 'Stay alert: the toad swallows whatever you say.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uACBD\uACC4\uD560 \uAC83. \uB450\uAEBC\uBE44\uB294 \uBB50\uB4E0
      \uB9D0\uD558\uB294 \uB300\uB85C \uC0BC\uD0A8\uB2E4."
    story: 202
    storyHeading: "85\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: alien
    english: alien
    meaning: "\uC678\uACC4\uC778, \uC678\uAD6D\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I feel like an alien among these stones.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uB3CC\uB4E4 \uC0AC\uC774\uC5D0\uC11C \uB098\uB294 \uC678\uACC4\uC778
      \uAC19\uB2E4."
    story: 221
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ambassador
    english: ambassador
    meaning: "\uB300\uC0AC, \uC0AC\uC808"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I am now the ambassador of the ministry to frogs.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uB098\uB294 \uBD80\uCC98\uAC00 \uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB4E4\uC5D0\uAC8C
      \uBCF4\uB0B8 \uC0AC\uC808\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 166
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ancient
    english: ancient
    meaning: "\uACE0\uB300\uC758, \uC544\uC8FC \uC624\uB798\uB41C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Ancient ruins stood in the green.
    exampleMeaning: "\uACE0\uB300 \uC720\uC801\uC774 \uCD08\uB85D \uC18D\uC5D0 \uC11C
      \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 174
    storyHeading: 
  - id: angle
    english: angle
    meaning: "\uAC01\uB3C4, \uAD00\uC810"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: From this angle, every tree looks the same.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uAC01\uB3C4\uC5D0\uC11C\uB294 \uB098\uBB34\uAC00 \uB2E4
      \uB611\uAC19\uC544 \uBCF4\uC778\uB2E4."
    story: 50
    storyHeading: 
  - id: anniversary
    english: anniversary
    meaning: "\uAE30\uB150\uC77C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Today is the anniversary of my last hot meal.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC624\uB298\uC740 \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9\uC73C\uB85C \uB530\uB73B\uD55C
      \uBC25\uC744 \uBA39\uC740 \uAE30\uB150\uC77C\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 115
    storyHeading: 
  - id: anxiety
    english: anxiety
    meaning: "\uBD88\uC548, \uAC71\uC815"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My anxiety grows with every word I lose.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9D0\uC744 \uC783\uC744 \uB54C\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uBD88\uC548\uC774
      \uCEE4\uC9C4\uB2E4."
    story: 67
    storyHeading: 
  - id: apparent
    english: apparent
    meaning: "\uBA85\uBC31\uD55C, \uAC89\uBCF4\uAE30\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: It was apparent that the letters were moving.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAE00\uC790\uB4E4\uC774 \uC6C0\uC9C1\uC774\uACE0 \uC788\uB294
      \uAC83\uC774 \uBA85\uBC31\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 69
    storyHeading: 
  - id: aspect
    english: aspect
    meaning: "\uCE21\uBA74, \uC591\uC0C1"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The worst aspect of the jungle is the singing frogs.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC815\uAE00\uC758 \uCD5C\uC545\uC758 \uCE21\uBA74\uC740 \uB178\uB798\uD558\uB294
      \uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB4E4\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 201
    storyHeading: 
  - id: assault
    english: assault
    meaning: "\uD3ED\uD589, \uACF5\uACA9"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule's assault on my map was sudden and complete.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC5D0 \uB300\uD55C \uB178\uC0C8\uC758 \uACF5\uACA9\uC740
      \uAC11\uC791\uC2A4\uB7FD\uACE0 \uC644\uBCBD\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 84
    storyHeading: 
  - id: auction
    english: auction
    meaning: "\uACBD\uB9E4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The cook held an auction for the last onion.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uB294 \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uC591\uD30C\uB97C
      \uACBD\uB9E4\uC5D0 \uBD80\uCCE4\uB2E4."
    story: 162
    storyHeading: 
  - id: author
    english: author
    meaning: "\uC791\uAC00, \uC800\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I am the author of the worst map ever made.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uB294 \uC5ED\uC0AC\uC0C1 \uCD5C\uC545\uC758 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC758
      \uC800\uC790\uB2E4."
    story: 195
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ban
    english: ban
    meaning: "\uAE08\uC9C0\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: We had to ban singing at night.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC24\uC5D0 \uB178\uB798\uD558\uB294 \uAC83\uC744 \uAE08\uC9C0\uD574\uC57C
      \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 164
    storyHeading: 
  - id: beast
    english: beast
    meaning: "\uC9D0\uC2B9, \uC57C\uC218"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A beast with question marks in its eyes stared at me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB208\uC5D0 \uBB3C\uC74C\uD45C\uAC00 \uB72C \uC9D0\uC2B9\uC774
      \uB098\uB97C \uCCD0\uB2E4\uBD24\uB2E4."
    story: 59
    storyHeading: 
  - id: behalf
    english: behalf
    meaning: "\uB300\uC2E0\uD568, \uC774\uC775"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: On behalf of the ministry, I apologize to this river.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uB97C \uB300\uC2E0\uD574 \uC774 \uAC15\uC5D0 \uC0AC\uACFC\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 167
    storyHeading: 
  - id: blast
    english: blast
    meaning: "\uD3ED\uBC1C, \uB3CC\uD48D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A blast of spores knocked me flat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD3EC\uC790 \uB3CC\uD48D\uC5D0 \uB098\uB294 \uB0A9\uC791\uD558\uAC8C
      \uC4F0\uB7EC\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 65
    storyHeading: 
  - id: bold
    english: bold
    meaning: "\uB300\uB2F4\uD55C, \uAD75\uC740"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: It was a bold plan, and it was also wrong.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB300\uB2F4\uD55C \uACC4\uD68D\uC774\uC5C8\uACE0, \uD2C0\uB9B0
      \uACC4\uD68D\uC774\uAE30\uB3C4 \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 263
    storyHeading: 
  - id: boost
    english: boost
    meaning: "\uBD81\uB3CB\uC6B0\uB2E4, \uB192\uC774\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: One warm meal can boost the whole crew.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB530\uB73B\uD55C \uBC25 \uD55C \uB07C\uB294 \uD300 \uC804\uCCB4\uB97C
      \uBD81\uB3CB\uC6B4\uB2E4."
    story: 264
    storyHeading: 
  - id: border
    english: border
    meaning: "\uAD6D\uACBD, \uACBD\uACC4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A stone marked the border of the forest.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB3CC \uD558\uB098\uAC00 \uC232\uC758 \uACBD\uACC4\uB97C \uD45C\uC2DC\uD558\uACE0
      \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 15
    storyHeading: 
  - id: broadcast
    english: broadcast
    meaning: "\uBC29\uC1A1\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: The frogs broadcast our location every night.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB4E4\uC740 \uB9E4\uC77C \uBC24 \uC6B0\uB9AC
      \uC704\uCE58\uB97C \uBC29\uC1A1\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 165
    storyHeading: 
  - id: bull
    english: bull
    meaning: "\uD669\uC18C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule thinks it is a bull.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC790\uAE30\uAC00 \uD669\uC18C\uC778 \uC904
      \uC548\uB2E4."
    story: 168
    storyHeading: 
  - id: cancer
    english: cancer
    meaning: "\uC554"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Doctors work hard to treat cancer.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC758\uC0AC\uB4E4\uC740 \uC554\uC744 \uCE58\uB8CC\uD558\uB824\uACE0
      \uC560\uC4F4\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: candidate
    english: candidate
    meaning: "\uD6C4\uBCF4\uC790, \uC9C0\uC6D0\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I am the only candidate for mayor of this rock.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uBC14\uC704\uC758 \uC2DC\uC7A5 \uD6C4\uBCF4\uB294 \uB098\uBFD0\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 143
    storyHeading: "55\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: capture
    english: capture
    meaning: "\uD3EC\uD68D\uD558\uB2E4, \uC0AC\uB85C\uC7A1\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: A giant toad tried to capture the cook's last word.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC70\uB300\uD55C \uB450\uAEBC\uBE44\uAC00 \uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uC758
      \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uB9D0\uC744 \uC0AC\uB85C\uC7A1\uC73C\uB824 \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 203
    storyHeading: 
  - id: celebrate
    english: celebrate
    meaning: "\uCD95\uD558\uD558\uB2E4, \uAE30\uB150\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: We celebrate with hot water and a boiled leaf.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB728\uAC70\uC6B4 \uBB3C\uACFC \uC0B6\uC740 \uC78E\uC73C\uB85C
      \uCD95\uD558\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 114
    storyHeading: 
  - id: celebrity
    english: celebrity
    meaning: "\uC720\uBA85 \uC778\uC0AC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: In this jungle, the mule is a celebrity.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC815\uAE00\uC5D0\uC11C \uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC720\uBA85
      \uC778\uC0AC\uB2E4."
    story: 169
    storyHeading: 
  - id: certificate
    english: certificate
    meaning: "\uC99D\uBA85\uC11C, \uC790\uACA9\uC99D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My certificate says I am an excellent mapmaker.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC99D\uBA85\uC11C\uC5D0\uB294 \uB0B4\uAC00 \uB6F0\uC5B4\uB09C
      \uC9C0\uB3C4 \uC81C\uC791\uC790\uB77C\uACE0 \uC801\uD600 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 3
    storyHeading: 
  - id: chamber
    english: chamber
    meaning: "\uBC29, \uD68C\uC758\uC2E4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The largest chamber was full of sleeping vines.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC00\uC7A5 \uD070 \uBC29\uC740 \uC7A0\uB4E0 \uB369\uAD74\uB85C
      \uAC00\uB4DD\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 279
    storyHeading: 
  - id: chaos
    english: chaos
    meaning: "\uD63C\uB3C8, \uD63C\uB780"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: By the third day, the crew was in chaos.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC0AC\uD758\uC9F8\uAC00 \uB418\uC790 \uD300\uC740 \uD63C\uB3C8\uC5D0
      \uBE60\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 21
    storyHeading: "3\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: charity
    english: charity
    meaning: "\uC790\uC120, \uC790\uC120 \uB2E8\uCCB4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The jungle gave me fruit out of charity.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC815\uAE00\uC740 \uC790\uC120\uD558\uB4EF \uB0B4\uAC8C \uC5F4\uB9E4\uB97C
      \uC8FC\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 157
    storyHeading: 
  - id: chemical
    english: chemical
    meaning: "\uD654\uD559\uC758, \uD654\uD559\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: A chemical smell rose from the black water.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC80\uC740 \uBB3C\uC5D0\uC11C \uD654\uD559 \uB0C4\uC0C8\uAC00
      \uC62C\uB77C\uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 271
    storyHeading: 
  - id: clay
    english: clay
    meaning: "\uC810\uD1A0, \uCC30\uD759"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I found a clay pot with letters pressed inside.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC548\uCABD\uC5D0 \uAE00\uC790\uAC00 \uCC0D\uD78C \uC810\uD1A0
      \uD56D\uC544\uB9AC\uB97C \uCC3E\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 188
    storyHeading: 
  - id: column
    english: column
    meaning: "\uAE30\uB465, \uCE7C\uB7FC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Each column held a sentence with a missing word.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAE30\uB465\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uB2E8\uC5B4\uAC00 \uBE60\uC9C4 \uBB38\uC7A5\uC774
      \uC0C8\uACA8\uC838 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 181
    storyHeading: 
  - id: combat
    english: combat
    meaning: "\uC804\uD22C, \uC2F8\uC6C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Combat with a snake in a knot is very confusing.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9E4\uB4ED\uC9C4 \uBC40\uACFC\uC758 \uC804\uD22C\uB294 \uC544\uC8FC
      \uD5F7\uAC08\uB9B0\uB2E4."
    story: 61
    storyHeading: 
  - id: commit
    english: commit
    meaning: "\uC800\uC9C0\uB974\uB2E4, \uC804\uB150\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I commit myself to one last page.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uD55C \uCABD\uC5D0 \uC804\uB150\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 285
    storyHeading: 
  - id: communist
    english: communist
    meaning: "\uACF5\uC0B0\uC8FC\uC758\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The old writer was a communist when young.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uB299\uC740 \uC791\uAC00\uB294 \uC80A\uC740 \uC2DC\uC808
      \uACF5\uC0B0\uC8FC\uC758\uC790\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: compete
    english: compete
    meaning: "\uACBD\uC7C1\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: My fears compete for my attention.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB450\uB824\uC6C0\uB4E4\uC774 \uB0B4 \uAD00\uC2EC\uC744 \uB450\uACE0
      \uACBD\uC7C1\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 274
    storyHeading: 
  - id: component
    english: component
    meaning: "\uAD6C\uC131 \uC694\uC18C, \uBD80\uD488"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: One component was missing, so I used my spoon.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uD488 \uD558\uB098\uAC00 \uC5C6\uC5B4\uC11C \uC21F\uAC00\uB77D\uC744
      \uC37C\uB2E4."
    story: 229
    storyHeading: 
  - id: concrete
    english: concrete
    meaning: "\uAD6C\uCCB4\uC801\uC778, \uCF58\uD06C\uB9AC\uD2B8\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I have no concrete plan, only a direction.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAD6C\uCCB4\uC801\uC778 \uACC4\uD68D\uC740 \uC5C6\uACE0 \uBC29\uD5A5\uB9CC
      \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 243
    storyHeading: 
  - id: conduct
    english: conduct
    meaning: "(\uC5C5\uBB34\uB97C) \uC218\uD589\uD558\uB2E4, \uC2E4\uC2DC\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I conduct my last survey by touch.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uCE21\uB7C9\uC740 \uC190\uC73C\uB85C \uB354\uB4EC\uC5B4
      \uC218\uD589\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 277
    storyHeading: 
  - id: congress
    english: congress
    meaning: "\uC758\uD68C, \uD68C\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A congress of frogs voted against me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC \uD68C\uC758\uAC00 \uB098\uB97C \uBC18\uB300\uD558\uAE30\uB85C
      \uD45C\uACB0\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 153
    storyHeading: 
  - id: consent
    english: consent
    meaning: "\uB3D9\uC758, \uD5C8\uB77D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule gave its consent by not biting me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uB098\uB97C \uBB3C\uC9C0 \uC54A\uB294 \uAC83\uC73C\uB85C
      \uB3D9\uC758\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 149
    storyHeading: 
  - id: contemporary
    english: contemporary
    meaning: "\uD604\uB300\uC758, \uB3D9\uC2DC\uB300\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: No contemporary map shows this place either.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB3D9\uC2DC\uB300\uC758 \uC5B4\uB5A4 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC5D0\uB3C4
      \uC774\uACF3\uC740 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 236
    storyHeading: 
  - id: contrast
    english: contrast
    meaning: "\uB300\uC870, \uB300\uBE44"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: In contrast, the cook blames the raccoon.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8\uC640 \uB300\uC870\uC801\uC73C\uB85C \uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uB294
      \uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC \uD0D3\uC744 \uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 200
    storyHeading: 
  - id: controversy
    english: controversy
    meaning: "\uB17C\uB780"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The biscuits caused a serious controversy.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBE44\uC2A4\uD0B7\uC740 \uC2EC\uAC01\uD55C \uB17C\uB780\uC744
      \uBD88\uB7EC\uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 24
    storyHeading: 
  - id: core
    english: core
    meaning: "\uD575\uC2EC, \uC911\uC2EC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: At the core of the vine, sentences were trapped.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB369\uAD74 \uC911\uC2EC\uC5D0 \uBB38\uC7A5\uB4E4\uC774 \uAC07\uD600
      \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 280
    storyHeading: 
  - id: corporate
    english: corporate
    meaning: "\uAE30\uC5C5\uC758, \uBC95\uC778\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My corporate manners died in the swamp.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uD68C\uC0AC \uC608\uC808\uC740 \uB2AA\uC5D0\uC11C \uC8FD\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 158
    storyHeading: 
  - id: courage
    english: courage
    meaning: "\uC6A9\uAE30"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Courage is easy when you have no other plan.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2EC\uB9AC \uACC4\uD68D\uC774 \uC5C6\uC73C\uBA74 \uC6A9\uAE30\uB294
      \uC27D\uB2E4."
    story: 64
    storyHeading: 
  - id: craft
    english: craft
    meaning: "\uACF5\uC608, \uAE30\uC220"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The craft of these people still stands in every stone.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC0AC\uB78C\uB4E4\uC758 \uAE30\uC220\uC740 \uBAA8\uB4E0
      \uB3CC\uC5D0 \uB0A8\uC544 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 187
    storyHeading: 
  - id: crew
    english: crew
    meaning: "\uC2B9\uBB34\uC6D0, \uD300"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My crew had five people and one very angry mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uD300\uC740 \uB2E4\uC12F \uBA85\uACFC \uC544\uC8FC
      \uD654\uB09C \uB178\uC0C8 \uD55C \uB9C8\uB9AC\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 6
    storyHeading: 
  - id: crucial
    english: crucial
    meaning: "\uACB0\uC815\uC801\uC778, \uC911\uB300\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: It is crucial to pull the lever before the bridge.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2E4\uB9AC \uC55E\uC5D0\uC11C \uB808\uBC84\uB97C \uB2F9\uAE30\uB294
      \uAC83\uC774 \uACB0\uC815\uC801\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 226
    storyHeading: 
  - id: crystal
    english: crystal
    meaning: "\uC218\uC815, \uACB0\uC815\uCCB4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A crystal glowed when I remembered a word.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9D0\uC744 \uB5A0\uC62C\uB9AC\uC790 \uC218\uC815\uC774 \uBE5B\uB0AC\uB2E4."
    story: 275
    storyHeading: 
  - id: currency
    english: currency
    meaning: "\uD1B5\uD654, \uD654\uD3D0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Here, the only currency is dry wood.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uD654\uD3D0\uB294 \uB9C8\uB978
      \uB098\uBB34\uB2E4."
    story: 161
    storyHeading: 
  - id: custom
    english: custom
    meaning: "\uAD00\uC2B5, \uD48D\uC2B5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The local custom seems to be leaving food at the door.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uACF3 \uD48D\uC2B5\uC740 \uBB38 \uC55E\uC5D0 \uC74C\uC2DD\uC744
      \uB450\uB294 \uAC83\uC778 \uB4EF\uD558\uB2E4."
    story: 185
    storyHeading: 
  - id: database
    english: database
    meaning: "\uB370\uC774\uD130\uBCA0\uC774\uC2A4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The school keeps its records in a database.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD559\uAD50\uB294 \uAE30\uB85D\uC744 \uB370\uC774\uD130\uBCA0\uC774\uC2A4\uC5D0
      \uBCF4\uAD00\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: decade
    english: decade
    meaning: "10\uB144"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Decade after decade, someone kept the stones clean.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC218\uC2ED \uB144 \uB3D9\uC548 \uB204\uAD70\uAC00 \uB3CC\uC744
      \uAE68\uB057\uC774 \uB2E6\uC544 \uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 184
    storyHeading: 
  - id: decent
    english: decent
    meaning: "\uAD1C\uCC2E\uC740, \uD488\uC704 \uC788\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: A decent blanket would make me cry.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAD1C\uCC2E\uC740 \uB2F4\uC694 \uD558\uB098\uBA74 \uC6B8 \uAC83
      \uAC19\uB2E4."
    story: 138
    storyHeading: 
  - id: decline
    english: decline
    meaning: "\uAC10\uC18C\uD558\uB2E4, \uAC70\uC808\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: My spelling began to decline in the third month.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC14B\uC9F8 \uB2EC\uBD80\uD130 \uB0B4 \uCCA0\uC790\uAC00 \uB5A8\uC5B4\uC9C0\uAE30
      \uC2DC\uC791\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 248
    storyHeading: 
  - id: defeat
    english: defeat
    meaning: "\uD328\uBC30\uC2DC\uD0A4\uB2E4, \uC774\uAE30\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: A ruler cannot defeat a snake.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC790\uB85C\uB294 \uBC40\uC744 \uC774\uAE38 \uC218 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 63
    storyHeading: 
  - id: defend
    english: defend
    meaning: "\uBC29\uC5B4\uD558\uB2E4, \uBCC0\uD638\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I tried to defend myself with a ruler.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC790\uB85C \uB098\uB97C \uBC29\uC5B4\uD558\uB824 \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 62
    storyHeading: 
  - id: democracy
    english: democracy
    meaning: "\uBBFC\uC8FC\uC8FC\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Democracy is hard when the mule has the only vote.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB9CC \uD45C\uB97C \uAC00\uC84C\uC73C\uBA74 \uBBFC\uC8FC\uC8FC\uC758\uB294
      \uC5B4\uB835\uB2E4."
    story: 154
    storyHeading: 
  - id: device
    english: device
    meaning: "\uC7A5\uCE58, \uAE30\uAE30"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I cannot explain this device; it just works.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC7A5\uCE58\uB294 \uC124\uBA85\uD560 \uC218 \uC5C6\uC9C0\uB9CC
      \uADF8\uB0E5 \uC791\uB3D9\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 228
    storyHeading: 
  - id: devil
    english: devil
    meaning: "\uC545\uB9C8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule is a devil, but it is my devil.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC545\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9CC, \uB0B4 \uC545\uB9C8\uB2E4."
    story: 91
    storyHeading: 
  - id: disaster
    english: disaster
    meaning: "\uC7AC\uB09C, \uC7AC\uD574"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We call that night the disaster of the soup.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC\uB294 \uADF8 \uBC24\uC744 \uC218\uD504 \uC7AC\uB09C\uC774\uB77C
      \uBD80\uB978\uB2E4."
    story: 77
    storyHeading: 
  - id: dispute
    english: dispute
    meaning: "\uBD84\uC7C1, \uB17C\uC7C1"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A dispute began over which way was north.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5B4\uB290 \uCABD\uC774 \uBD81\uCABD\uC778\uC9C0\uB97C \uB450\uACE0
      \uBD84\uC7C1\uC774 \uC2DC\uC791\uB418\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 22
    storyHeading: 
  - id: distinct
    english: distinct
    meaning: "\uB69C\uB837\uD55C, \uBCC4\uAC1C\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: 'I heard one distinct word: home.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB69C\uB837\uD55C \uD55C\uB9C8\uB514\uAC00 \uB4E4\uB838\uB2E4.
      \uC9D1."
    story: 127
    storyHeading: 
  - id: domain
    english: domain
    meaning: "\uC601\uC5ED, \uBD84\uC57C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The toad's domain ends at the falls.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB450\uAEBC\uBE44\uC758 \uC601\uC5ED\uC740 \uD3ED\uD3EC\uC5D0\uC11C
      \uB05D\uB09C\uB2E4."
    story: 266
    storyHeading: 
  - id: draft
    english: draft
    meaning: "\uCD08\uC548, \uC6D0\uACE0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This is only a draft; please excuse the mud.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uAC74 \uCD08\uC548\uC77C \uBFD0\uC774\uB2E4. \uC9C4\uD759\uC740
      \uC591\uD574\uD574 \uC8FC\uAE38."
    story: 31
    storyHeading: 
  - id: dual
    english: dual
    meaning: "\uC774\uC911\uC758, \uB458\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: It had a dual voice, mine and something worse.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8\uAC83\uC740 \uC774\uC911\uC758 \uBAA9\uC18C\uB9AC\uB97C
      \uB0C8\uB2E4. \uB0B4 \uAC83\uACFC \uB354 \uB098\uC05C \uAC83."
    story: 126
    storyHeading: 
  - id: dynamic
    english: dynamic
    meaning: "\uC5ED\uB3D9\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: 'Our team is dynamic: we run in all directions.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uD300\uC740 \uC5ED\uB3D9\uC801\uC774\uB2E4. \uC0AC\uBC29\uC73C\uB85C
      \uB2EC\uB9B0\uB2E4."
    story: 141
    storyHeading: 
  - id: efficient
    english: efficient
    meaning: "\uD6A8\uC728\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The most efficient way to cross is to fall in.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC74\uB108\uB294 \uAC00\uC7A5 \uD6A8\uC728\uC801\uC778 \uBC29\uBC95\uC740
      \uBE60\uC9C0\uB294 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 142
    storyHeading: 
  - id: electronic
    english: electronic
    meaning: "\uC804\uC790\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Electronic books are easy to carry.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC804\uC790\uCC45\uC740 \uB4E4\uACE0 \uB2E4\uB2C8\uAE30 \uC27D\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: elite
    english: elite
    meaning: "\uC5D8\uB9AC\uD2B8, \uC815\uC608"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The elite of the ministry have never seen a swamp.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uC758 \uC5D8\uB9AC\uD2B8\uB4E4\uC740 \uB2AA\uC744
      \uBCF8 \uC801\uC774 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 214
    storyHeading: 
  - id: emergency
    english: emergency
    meaning: "\uBE44\uC0C1\uC0AC\uD0DC, \uC751\uAE09"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I declared an emergency. The mule did not care.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uB294 \uBE44\uC0C1\uC0AC\uD0DC\uB97C \uC120\uC5B8\uD588\uB2E4.
      \uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC2E0\uACBD \uC4F0\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 28
    storyHeading: 
  - id: encounter
    english: encounter
    meaning: "\uB9C8\uC8FC\uCE58\uB2E4, \uB9DE\uB2E5\uB728\uB9AC\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I did not expect to encounter a raccoon with a signpost.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC815\uD45C\uB97C \uB4E0 \uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB97C \uB9C8\uC8FC\uCE60
      \uC904\uC740 \uBAB0\uB790\uB2E4."
    story: 54
    storyHeading: "12\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: enterprise
    english: enterprise
    meaning: "\uAE30\uC5C5, \uC0AC\uC5C5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This whole enterprise was a mistake.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC0AC\uC5C5 \uC804\uCCB4\uAC00 \uC2E4\uC218\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 159
    storyHeading: 
  - id: entry
    english: entry
    meaning: "\uC785\uC7A5, \uCD9C\uC785"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The sign said no entry, but the letters were mixed up.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD45C\uC9C0\uD310\uC5D0\uB294 \uCD9C\uC785 \uAE08\uC9C0\uB77C
      \uC801\uD614\uC9C0\uB9CC \uAE00\uC790\uAC00 \uB4A4\uC11E\uC5EC \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 16
    storyHeading: 
  - id: era
    english: era
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uB300"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Those words came from another era.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uB9D0\uB4E4\uC740 \uB2E4\uB978 \uC2DC\uB300\uC5D0\uC11C
      \uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 183
    storyHeading: 
  - id: estate
    english: estate
    meaning: "\uBD80\uB3D9\uC0B0, \uC0AC\uC720\uC9C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My small estate back home can go to the mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uACE0\uD5A5\uC758 \uC791\uC740 \uB545\uC740 \uB178\uC0C8\uC5D0\uAC8C
      \uB118\uAE34\uB2E4."
    story: 100
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ethnic
    english: ethnic
    meaning: "\uBBFC\uC871\uC758, \uC778\uC885\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The city has many ethnic restaurants.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uB3C4\uC2DC\uC5D0\uB294 \uC5EC\uB7EC \uBBFC\uC871 \uC74C\uC2DD\uC810\uC774
      \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: evolution
    english: evolution
    meaning: "\uC9C4\uD654, \uBC1C\uC804"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The evolution of my diet is frightening.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC2DD\uB2E8\uC758 \uBC1C\uC804\uC740 \uBB34\uC12D\uB2E4."
    story: 250
    storyHeading: 
  - id: executive
    english: executive
    meaning: "\uACBD\uC601 \uAC04\uBD80, \uC784\uC6D0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: An executive shook my hand and forgot my name.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC04\uBD80 \uD55C \uC0AC\uB78C\uC774 \uB0B4 \uC190\uC744 \uC7A1\uB354\uB2C8
      \uC774\uB984\uC744 \uC78A\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 11
    storyHeading: 
  - id: explore
    english: explore
    meaning: "\uD0D0\uD5D8\uD558\uB2E4, \uD0D0\uAD6C\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I went to explore the swamp alone.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD63C\uC790 \uB2AA\uC744 \uD0D0\uD5D8\uD558\uB7EC \uAC14\uB2E4."
    story: 33
    storyHeading: "7\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: extent
    english: extent
    meaning: "\uC815\uB3C4, \uBC94\uC704"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Nobody knows the extent of the forest.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC232\uC774 \uC5B4\uB514\uAE4C\uC9C0\uC778\uC9C0 \uADF8 \uBC94\uC704\uB97C
      \uC544\uBB34\uB3C4 \uBAA8\uB978\uB2E4."
    story: 110
    storyHeading: 
  - id: external
    english: external
    meaning: "\uC678\uBD80\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Soon no external light reached us.
    exampleMeaning: "\uACE7 \uBC14\uAE65 \uBE5B\uC774 \uB2FF\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 269
    storyHeading: 
  - id: extraordinary
    english: extraordinary
    meaning: "\uD2B9\uBCC4\uD55C, \uBE44\uBC94\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Its tongue is extraordinary, and so is its appetite.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uD600\uB294 \uD2B9\uBCC4\uD558\uACE0, \uC2DD\uC695\uB3C4
      \uADF8\uB807\uB2E4."
    story: 204
    storyHeading: 
  - id: facility
    english: facility
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uC124"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The only facility here is a very private bush.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uC2DC\uC124\uC740 \uC544\uC8FC
      \uC740\uBC00\uD55C \uB364\uBD88\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 132
    storyHeading: 
  - id: faculty
    english: faculty
    meaning: "\uB2A5\uB825, \uAD50\uC218\uC9C4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I will send the whole faculty a very muddy letter.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAD50\uC218\uC9C4 \uC804\uCCB4\uC5D0\uAC8C \uC544\uC8FC \uC9C4\uD759
      \uBB3B\uC740 \uD3B8\uC9C0\uB97C \uBCF4\uB0BC \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 218
    storyHeading: 
  - id: false
    english: false
    meaning: "\uAC70\uC9D3\uC758, \uD2C0\uB9B0"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Every path I trust turns out to be false.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBBFF\uC740 \uAE38\uC740 \uBAA8\uB450 \uAC70\uC9D3\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 72
    storyHeading: 
  - id: fame
    english: fame
    meaning: "\uBA85\uC131"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I wanted fame; now I want soup.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBA85\uC131\uC744 \uC6D0\uD588\uC9C0\uB9CC, \uC774\uC81C\uB294
      \uC218\uD504\uB97C \uC6D0\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 213
    storyHeading: 
  - id: fate
    english: fate
    meaning: "\uC6B4\uBA85, \uC219\uBA85"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'The cook met a strange fate: every word ended in dots.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uC758 \uC6B4\uBA85\uC740 \uC774\uC0C1\uD588\uB2E4.
      \uBAA8\uB4E0 \uB9D0\uC774 \uC810\uC73C\uB85C \uB05D\uB0AC\uB2E4."
    story: 282
    storyHeading: 
  - id: federal
    english: federal
    meaning: "\uC5F0\uBC29\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: No federal law covers this.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uAC74 \uC5B4\uB5A4 \uC5F0\uBC29\uBC95\uC5D0\uB3C4 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 155
    storyHeading: 
  - id: fiction
    english: fiction
    meaning: "\uC18C\uC124, \uD5C8\uAD6C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: It may be fiction, but I wrote it down.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD5C8\uAD6C\uC77C\uC9C0 \uBAB0\uB77C\uB3C4 \uC801\uC5B4 \uB450\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 193
    storyHeading: 
  - id: filter
    english: filter
    meaning: "\uC5EC\uACFC\uAE30, \uD544\uD130"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I made a filter from my last clean sock.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uB0A8\uC740 \uAE68\uB057\uD55C \uC591\uB9D0\uB85C
      \uC5EC\uACFC\uAE30\uB97C \uB9CC\uB4E4\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 39
    storyHeading: 
  - id: format
    english: format
    meaning: "\uD615\uC2DD, \uAD6C\uC131"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The ministry wants a proper format, even here.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uB294 \uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C\uB3C4 \uC81C\uB300\uB85C
      \uB41C \uD615\uC2DD\uC744 \uC6D0\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 32
    storyHeading: 
  - id: former
    english: former
    meaning: "\uC774\uC804\uC758, \uC804\uC790\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My former life had chairs.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC774\uC804 \uC0B6\uC5D0\uB294 \uC758\uC790\uAC00 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 237
    storyHeading: 
  - id: formula
    english: formula
    meaning: "\uACF5\uC2DD, \uBC29\uC2DD"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'I worked out a formula: lost plus hungry equals poet.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uACF5\uC2DD\uC744 \uC138\uC6E0\uB2E4. \uAE38 \uC783\uC74C \uB354\uD558\uAE30
      \uBC30\uACE0\uD514\uC740 \uC2DC\uC778."
    story: 234
    storyHeading: 
  - id: foster
    english: foster
    meaning: "\uC721\uC131\uD558\uB2E4, \uCD09\uC9C4\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: This jungle will foster patience or madness.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC815\uAE00\uC740 \uC778\uB0B4\uC2EC\uC774\uB098 \uAD11\uAE30\uB97C
      \uD0A4\uC6B4\uB2E4."
    story: 246
    storyHeading: 
  - id: framework
    english: framework
    meaning: "\uD2C0, \uCCB4\uACC4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I drew a framework for a new map from memory.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAE30\uC5B5\uC73C\uB85C \uC0C8 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC758 \uD2C0\uC744
      \uADF8\uB838\uB2E4."
    story: 233
    storyHeading: "100\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: frequent
    english: frequent
    meaning: "\uC7A6\uC740, \uBE48\uBC88\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Frequent rain turned my notes to soup.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC7A6\uC740 \uBE44\uC5D0 \uB0B4 \uAE30\uB85D\uC774 \uC218\uD504\uAC00
      \uB418\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 123
    storyHeading: 
  - id: fuel
    english: fuel
    meaning: "\uC5F0\uB8CC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Wet wood is poor fuel, but my old reports burn well.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC816\uC740 \uB098\uBB34\uB294 \uD615\uD3B8\uC5C6\uB294 \uC5F0\uB8CC\uC9C0\uB9CC
      \uC61B \uBCF4\uACE0\uC11C\uB294 \uC798 \uD0C4\uB2E4."
    story: 41
    storyHeading: 
  - id: fundamental
    english: fundamental
    meaning: "\uADFC\uBCF8\uC801\uC778, \uAE30\uBCF8\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: 'A fundamental rule: never eat what winks at you.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uADFC\uBCF8\uC801\uC778 \uADDC\uCE59. \uC719\uD06C\uD558\uB294
      \uAC83\uC740 \uBA39\uC9C0 \uB9D0 \uAC83."
    story: 75
    storyHeading: 
  - id: funeral
    english: funeral
    meaning: "\uC7A5\uB840\uC2DD"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We held a small funeral for my last pen.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uD39C\uC758 \uC791\uC740 \uC7A5\uB840\uC2DD\uC744
      \uCE58\uB800\uB2E4."
    story: 283
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gallery
    english: gallery
    meaning: "\uBBF8\uC220\uAD00, \uD654\uB791"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: One room was a gallery of blank stone faces.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C \uBC29\uC740 \uBE48 \uB3CC \uC5BC\uAD74\uB4E4\uC758 \uD654\uB791\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 189
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gang
    english: gang
    meaning: "\uBB34\uB9AC, \uAC31\uB2E8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A gang of mushrooms blocked the path.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC84\uC12F \uBB34\uB9AC\uAC00 \uAE38\uC744 \uB9C9\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 58
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gap
    english: gap
    meaning: "\uD2C8, \uACA9\uCC28"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Through a gap in the trees I saw a river.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uBB34 \uD2C8\uC73C\uB85C \uAC15\uC774 \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 111
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gender
    english: gender
    meaning: "\uC131\uBCC4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The form asks for your name and gender.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC11C\uB958\uB294 \uC774\uB984\uACFC \uC131\uBCC4\uC744 \uBB3B\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gene
    english: gene
    meaning: "\uC720\uC804\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This gene affects the color of your eyes.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC720\uC804\uC790\uB294 \uB208 \uC0C9\uAE54\uC5D0 \uC601\uD5A5\uC744
      \uC900\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: genius
    english: genius
    meaning: "\uCC9C\uC7AC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I am no genius, but I can climb when afraid.
    exampleMeaning: "\uCC9C\uC7AC\uB294 \uC544\uB2C8\uC9C0\uB9CC, \uBB34\uC11C\uC6B0\uBA74
      \uB098\uBB34\uB294 \uD0C4\uB2E4."
    story: 207
    storyHeading: 
  - id: genuine
    english: genuine
    meaning: "\uC9C4\uC9DC\uC758, \uC9C4\uC2E4\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Its kindness seemed genuine; it dropped me a fruit.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uCE5C\uC808\uC740 \uC9C4\uC9DC \uAC19\uC558\uB2E4. \uC5F4\uB9E4\uB97C
      \uB5A8\uC5B4\uB728\uB824 \uC8FC\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 103
    storyHeading: 
  - id: globe
    english: globe
    meaning: "\uC9C0\uAD6C, \uAD6C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I once owned a globe. The world looked smaller then.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C\uB54C \uC9C0\uAD6C\uBCF8\uC774 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4. \uADF8\uB54C\uB294
      \uC138\uC0C1\uC774 \uB354 \uC791\uC544 \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 239
    storyHeading: 
  - id: graduate
    english: graduate
    meaning: "\uC878\uC5C5\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I will graduate from the academy this spring.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC62C\uBD04\uC5D0 \uB098\uB294 \uD559\uC6D0\uC744 \uC878\uC5C5\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 1
    storyHeading: "\uCD9C\uBC1C \uC804"
  - id: grateful
    english: grateful
    meaning: "\uAC10\uC0AC\uD558\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I am grateful for dry socks, the rarest treasure.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uB978 \uC591\uB9D0\uC5D0 \uAC10\uC0AC\uD55C\uB2E4. \uAC00\uC7A5
      \uADC0\uD55C \uBCF4\uBB3C\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 97
    storyHeading: 
  - id: grave
    english: grave
    meaning: "\uBB34\uB364"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I found a grave with no name, only three dots.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uB984 \uC5C6\uC774 \uC810 \uC138 \uAC1C\uB9CC \uC788\uB294
      \uBB34\uB364\uC744 \uCC3E\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 281
    storyHeading: 
  - id: gross
    english: gross
    meaning: "\uCD1D\uACC4\uC758, \uC5ED\uACA8\uC6B4"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The swamp water tastes gross.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2AA \uBB3C\uC740 \uC5ED\uACA8\uC6B4 \uB9DB\uC774 \uB09C\uB2E4."
    story: 37
    storyHeading: 
  - id: guardian
    english: guardian
    meaning: "\uBCF4\uD638\uC790, \uC218\uD638\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: An old tree seems to be the guardian of this path.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB299\uC740 \uB098\uBB34\uAC00 \uC774 \uAE38\uC758 \uC218\uD638\uC790\uC778
      \uAC83 \uAC19\uB2E4."
    story: 102
    storyHeading: "30\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: gulf
    english: gulf
    meaning: "\uB9CC(\u7063), \uACA9\uCC28"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The river opened into a wide gulf of brown water.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC15\uC740 \uB113\uC740 \uD759\uD0D5\uBB3C \uB9CC\uC73C\uB85C
      \uD2B8\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 121
    storyHeading: 
  - id: headquarters
    english: headquarters
    meaning: "\uBCF8\uC0AC, \uBCF8\uBD80"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Headquarters promised we would be home in a week.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBCF8\uBD80\uB294 \uC77C\uC8FC\uC77C\uC774\uBA74 \uC9D1\uC5D0
      \uB3CC\uC544\uC628\uB2E4\uACE0 \uC57D\uC18D\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 10
    storyHeading: 
  - id: heritage
    english: heritage
    meaning: "\uC720\uC0B0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This heritage belongs to nobody now.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC720\uC0B0\uC740 \uC774\uC81C \uB204\uAD6C\uC758 \uAC83\uB3C4
      \uC544\uB2C8\uB2E4."
    story: 182
    storyHeading: 
  - id: holy
    english: holy
    meaning: "\uC2E0\uC131\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The place felt holy, so I took off my muddy hat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC2E0\uC131\uD55C \uACF3 \uAC19\uC544\uC11C \uC9C4\uD759 \uBB3B\uC740
      \uBAA8\uC790\uB97C \uBC97\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 176
    storyHeading: 
  - id: hook
    english: hook
    meaning: "\uAC08\uACE0\uB9AC, \uAC78\uC774"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I bent a hook from my pen and caught nothing.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD39C\uC744 \uAD6C\uBD80\uB824 \uAC08\uACE0\uB9AC\uB97C \uB9CC\uB4E4\uC5C8\uC9C0\uB9CC
      \uC544\uBB34\uAC83\uB3C4 \uBABB \uC7A1\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 43
    storyHeading: 
  - id: horror
    english: horror
    meaning: "\uACF5\uD3EC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'I read my own report with horror: half was gone.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uBCF4\uACE0\uC11C\uB97C \uC77D\uACE0 \uACF5\uD3EC\uC5D0
      \uB5A8\uC5C8\uB2E4. \uC808\uBC18\uC774 \uC0AC\uB77C\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 68
    storyHeading: 
  - id: host
    english: host
    meaning: "\uC8FC\uC778, \uC9C4\uD589\uC790"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Our host was a very old turtle.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uC8FC\uC778\uC740 \uC544\uC8FC \uB299\uC740 \uAC70\uBD81\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 133
    storyHeading: 
  - id: household
    english: household
    meaning: "\uAC00\uC815, \uAC00\uAD6C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: In my household, the kettle sang every morning.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uC9D1\uC5D0\uC11C\uB294 \uC544\uCE68\uB9C8\uB2E4
      \uC8FC\uC804\uC790\uAC00 \uB178\uB798\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 101
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ideal
    english: ideal
    meaning: "\uC774\uC0C1\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: This is not an ideal place to sleep.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uB294 \uC7A0\uC790\uAE30\uC5D0 \uC774\uC0C1\uC801\uC778
      \uACF3\uC774 \uC544\uB2C8\uB2E4."
    story: 137
    storyHeading: 
  - id: impact
    english: impact
    meaning: "\uC601\uD5A5, \uCDA9\uACA9"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The impact made me forget the word for shoe.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uCDA9\uACA9\uC73C\uB85C \uC2E0\uBC1C\uC774\uB77C\uB294
      \uB9D0\uC744 \uC78A\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 66
    storyHeading: 
  - id: incident
    english: incident
    meaning: "\uC0AC\uAC74"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The cook blames the incident on the raccoon.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uB294 \uADF8 \uC0AC\uAC74\uC744 \uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC
      \uD0D3\uC73C\uB85C \uB3CC\uB9B0\uB2E4."
    story: 78
    storyHeading: 
  - id: index
    english: index
    meaning: "\uC0C9\uC778, \uC9C0\uC218"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I made an index of all the trees. It did not help.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uBB34\uB97C \uC804\uBD80 \uC0C9\uC778\uC73C\uB85C \uB9CC\uB4E4\uC5C8\uB2E4.
      \uC18C\uC6A9\uC5C6\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 51
    storyHeading: 
  - id: initial
    english: initial
    meaning: "\uCC98\uC74C\uC758, \uCD08\uAE30\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My initial plan had a lunch break.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uCC98\uC74C \uACC4\uD68D\uC5D0\uB294 \uC810\uC2EC\uC2DC\uAC04\uC774
      \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 242
    storyHeading: 
  - id: input
    english: input
    meaning: "\uC785\uB825, \uD22C\uC785"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule's input was a long, rude noise.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uAC00 \uBCF4\uD0E0 \uAC83\uC740 \uAE38\uACE0 \uBB34\uB840\uD55C
      \uC18C\uB9AC\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 253
    storyHeading: 
  - id: institute
    english: institute
    meaning: "\uC5F0\uAD6C\uC18C, \uAE30\uAD00"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The institute will not believe a word of this.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5F0\uAD6C\uC18C\uB294 \uC774 \uB9D0\uC744 \uD55C \uB9C8\uB514\uB3C4
      \uBBFF\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC744 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 219
    storyHeading: 
  - id: intelligent
    english: intelligent
    meaning: "\uCD1D\uBA85\uD55C, \uC9C0\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The toad is more intelligent than my old boss.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB450\uAEBC\uBE44\uB294 \uB0B4 \uC61B \uC0C1\uC0AC\uBCF4\uB2E4
      \uCD1D\uBA85\uD558\uB2E4."
    story: 208
    storyHeading: 
  - id: interior
    english: interior
    meaning: "\uB0B4\uBD80, \uC2E4\uB0B4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The interior of the forest has no sky.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC232 \uC548\uCABD\uC5D0\uB294 \uD558\uB298\uC774 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 105
    storyHeading: 
  - id: jail
    english: jail
    meaning: "\uAC10\uC625"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Our jail was a circle of sticks; nobody stayed in it.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uAC10\uC625\uC740 \uB098\uBB47\uAC00\uC9C0 \uC6D0\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4.
      \uC544\uBB34\uB3C4 \uC548 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 82
    storyHeading: 
  - id: joint
    english: joint
    meaning: "\uAD00\uC808, \uC774\uC74C\uB9E4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Every joint in my body hurts.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBAB8\uC758 \uAD00\uC808\uC774 \uB2E4 \uC544\uD504\uB2E4."
    story: 230
    storyHeading: 
  - id: journal
    english: journal
    meaning: "\uC77C\uC9C0, \uD559\uC220\uC9C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This journal is now my only official report.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uC774 \uC77C\uC9C0\uAC00 \uB0B4 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C
      \uACF5\uC2DD \uBCF4\uACE0\uC11C\uB2E4."
    story: 29
    storyHeading: 
  - id: jury
    english: jury
    meaning: "\uBC30\uC2EC\uC6D0\uB2E8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We held a trial. The jury was two men and a mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC7AC\uD310\uC744 \uC5F4\uC5C8\uB2E4. \uBC30\uC2EC\uC6D0\uB2E8\uC740
      \uB450 \uC0AC\uB78C\uACFC \uB178\uC0C8\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 80
    storyHeading: 
  - id: kit
    english: kit
    meaning: "\uB3C4\uAD6C \uD55C \uBC8C, \uD0A4\uD2B8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I packed a full kit of pens, rope and biscuits.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD39C\uACFC \uBC27\uC904\uACFC \uBE44\uC2A4\uD0B7\uC73C\uB85C
      \uB3C4\uAD6C \uD55C \uBC8C\uC744 \uAFB8\uB838\uB2E4."
    story: 7
    storyHeading: 
  - id: knight
    english: knight
    meaning: "\uAE30\uC0AC(\u9A0E\u58EB)"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I found a rusted knight standing guard, empty inside.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC18D\uC774 \uBE48 \uB179\uC2A8 \uAE30\uC0AC\uAC00 \uBCF4\uCD08\uB97C
      \uC11C\uACE0 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 278
    storyHeading: 
  - id: landscape
    english: landscape
    meaning: "\uD48D\uACBD, \uACBD\uCE58"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The landscape was so green it hurt my eyes.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD48D\uACBD\uC740 \uB208\uC774 \uC544\uD50C \uB9CC\uD07C \uD478\uB974\uB800\uB2E4."
    story: 13
    storyHeading: 
  - id: latter
    english: latter
    meaning: "\uD6C4\uC790\uC758, \uD6C4\uBC18\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Mud or rain? I choose the latter, and get both.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C4\uD759\uC774\uB0D0 \uBE44\uB0D0? \uD6C4\uC790\uB97C \uACE8\uB790\uB294\uB370
      \uB458 \uB2E4 \uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 241
    storyHeading: 
  - id: launch
    english: launch
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uC791\uD558\uB2E4, \uBC1C\uC0AC\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: We launch it at dawn.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC0C8\uBCBD\uC5D0 \uBC30\uB97C \uB744\uC6B4\uB2E4."
    story: 119
    storyHeading: 
  - id: leather
    english: leather
    meaning: "\uAC00\uC8FD"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I ate part of my leather boot. It was not bad.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC00\uC8FD \uC7A5\uD654\uB97C \uC870\uAE08 \uBA39\uC5C8\uB2E4.
      \uB098\uC058\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 42
    storyHeading: 
  - id: legend
    english: legend
    meaning: "\uC804\uC124"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A legend says the first word was spoken here.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC804\uC124\uC5D0 \uB530\uB974\uBA74 \uCCAB \uB9D0\uC774 \uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C
      \uB098\uC654\uB2E4."
    story: 192
    storyHeading: 
  - id: liberal
    english: liberal
    meaning: "\uC790\uC720\uC8FC\uC758\uC758, \uC9C4\uBCF4\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The newspaper has a liberal point of view.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uC2E0\uBB38\uC740 \uC9C4\uBCF4\uC801\uC778 \uAD00\uC810\uC744
      \uAC00\uC9C0\uACE0 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: liberty
    english: liberty
    meaning: "\uC790\uC720"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: It then took the liberty of eating the treaty.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uACE7 \uC81C \uB9C8\uC74C\uB300\uB85C \uC870\uC57D\uC744
      \uBA39\uC5B4 \uBC84\uB838\uB2E4."
    story: 150
    storyHeading: 
  - id: liquid
    english: liquid
    meaning: "\uC561\uCCB4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I drank a black liquid and lived.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC80\uC740 \uC561\uCCB4\uB97C \uB9C8\uC2DC\uACE0\uB3C4 \uC0B4\uC544\uB0A8\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 38
    storyHeading: 
  - id: literature
    english: literature
    meaning: "\uBB38\uD559"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Hunger is very bad for literature.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC30\uACE0\uD514\uC740 \uBB38\uD559\uC5D0 \uC544\uC8FC \uB098\uC058\uB2E4."
    story: 196
    storyHeading: 
  - id: logic
    english: logic
    meaning: "\uB17C\uB9AC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Logic does not work in this forest.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC232\uC5D0\uC11C\uB294 \uB17C\uB9AC\uAC00 \uD1B5\uD558\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 71
    storyHeading: 
  - id: luxury
    english: luxury
    meaning: "\uC0AC\uCE58, \uD638\uD654\uB85C\uC6C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Sleep is a luxury here.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C \uC7A0\uC740 \uC0AC\uCE58\uB2E4."
    story: 98
    storyHeading: 
  - id: margin
    english: margin
    meaning: "\uC5EC\uBC31, \uCC28\uC774"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I write in the margin to save paper.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC885\uC774\uB97C \uC544\uB07C\uB824\uACE0 \uC5EC\uBC31\uC5D0
      \uC4F4\uB2E4."
    story: 52
    storyHeading: 
  - id: marine
    english: marine
    meaning: "\uBC14\uB2E4\uC758, \uD574\uC591\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The air smelled almost marine, salty and thick.
    exampleMeaning: "\uACF5\uAE30\uB294 \uBC14\uB2E4 \uB0C4\uC0C8\uAC00 \uB0A0 \uB9CC\uD07C
      \uC9DC\uACE0 \uC9C4\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 117
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mature
    english: mature
    meaning: "\uC131\uC219\uD55C, \uB2E4 \uC790\uB780"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The cook says I am more mature now. I bit a frog.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uB294 \uB0B4\uAC00 \uC131\uC219\uD574\uC84C\uB2E4
      \uD55C\uB2E4. \uB098\uB294 \uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB97C \uBB3C\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 140
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mayor
    english: mayor
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uC7A5(\u5E02\u9577)"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I won. I am now the mayor of a wet rock.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2F9\uC120\uB410\uB2E4. \uC774\uC81C \uB098\uB294 \uC816\uC740
      \uBC14\uC704\uC758 \uC2DC\uC7A5\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 144
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mechanism
    english: mechanism
    meaning: "\uAE30\uACC4 \uC7A5\uCE58, \uBC29\uBC95"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: An old mechanism lowered a vine bridge.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC61B \uAE30\uACC4 \uC7A5\uCE58\uAC00 \uB369\uAD74 \uB2E4\uB9AC\uB97C
      \uB0B4\uB838\uB2E4."
    story: 227
    storyHeading: 
  - id: medium
    english: medium
    meaning: "\uB9E4\uCCB4, \uC218\uB2E8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Paper is a poor medium in this rain.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uBE44\uC5D0 \uC885\uC774\uB294 \uD615\uD3B8\uC5C6\uB294
      \uC218\uB2E8\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 129
    storyHeading: 
  - id: million
    english: million
    meaning: "100\uB9CC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: There are a million frogs and one of me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB294 \uBC31\uB9CC \uB9C8\uB9AC\uACE0 \uB098\uB294
      \uD558\uB098\uB2E4."
    story: 254
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mine
    english: mine
    meaning: "\uAD11\uC0B0, \uC9C0\uB8B0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Down there was an old mine, black as ink.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uC544\uB798\uB294 \uC789\uD06C\uCC98\uB7FC \uAC80\uC740
      \uC61B \uAD11\uC0B0\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 270
    storyHeading: 
  - id: minimum
    english: minimum
    meaning: "\uCD5C\uC18C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I eat the minimum now and write the maximum.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uB098\uB294 \uCD5C\uC18C\uD55C\uB9CC \uBA39\uACE0
      \uCD5C\uB300\uD55C \uC801\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 27
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ministry
    english: ministry
    meaning: "(\uC815\uBD80\uC758) \uBD80\uCC98"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The ministry sent me to map the great forest.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uB294 \uD070 \uC232\uC758 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uB97C \uADF8\uB9AC\uB77C\uBA70
      \uB098\uB97C \uBCF4\uB0C8\uB2E4."
    story: 4
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mobile
    english: mobile
    meaning: "\uC774\uB3D9\uC2DD\uC758, \uC6C0\uC9C1\uC774\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Our mobile kitchen is a pot on a mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uC774\uB3D9\uC2DD \uBD80\uC5CC\uC740 \uB178\uC0C8
      \uC704\uC758 \uB0C4\uBE44\uB2E4."
    story: 131
    storyHeading: 
  - id: mode
    english: mode
    meaning: "\uBC29\uC2DD, \uBAA8\uB4DC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The crew is in survival mode now.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD300\uC740 \uC774\uC81C \uC0DD\uC874 \uBAA8\uB4DC\uB2E4."
    story: 130
    storyHeading: 
  - id: moderate
    english: moderate
    meaning: "\uC801\uB2F9\uD55C, \uC628\uAC74\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Moderate rain here means a small flood.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C \uC801\uB2F9\uD55C \uBE44\uB780 \uC791\uC740
      \uD64D\uC218\uB97C \uB73B\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 139
    storyHeading: 
  - id: monster
    english: monster
    meaning: "\uAD34\uBB3C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Another monster asked me to spell my own name.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2E4\uB978 \uAD34\uBB3C\uC740 \uB0B4 \uC774\uB984\uC758 \uCCA0\uC790\uB97C
      \uB300 \uBCF4\uB77C\uACE0 \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 60
    storyHeading: 
  - id: multiple
    english: multiple
    meaning: "\uB2E4\uC218\uC758, \uC5EC\uB7EC"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I have multiple holes in my hat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBAA8\uC790\uC5D0 \uAD6C\uBA4D\uC774 \uC5EC\uB7EC \uAC1C \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 256
    storyHeading: 
  - id: murder
    english: murder
    meaning: "\uC0B4\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: There was nearly a murder over the last spoon of soup.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uC218\uD504 \uD55C \uC21F\uAC08\uC5D0 \uD558\uB9C8\uD130\uBA74
      \uC0B4\uC778\uC774 \uB0A0 \uBED4\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 83
    storyHeading: 
  - id: naked
    english: naked
    meaning: "\uBC8C\uAC70\uBC97\uC740"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I crossed the river naked to keep my papers dry.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC11C\uB958\uB97C \uC9C0\uD0A4\uB824\uACE0 \uBC8C\uAC70\uBC97\uACE0
      \uAC15\uC744 \uAC74\uB11C\uB2E4."
    story: 86
    storyHeading: 
  - id: network
    english: network
    meaning: "\uB9DD, \uB124\uD2B8\uC6CC\uD06C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A network of roots covers the ground.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBFCC\uB9AC\uB9DD\uC774 \uB545\uC744 \uB36E\uACE0 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 261
    storyHeading: 
  - id: novel
    english: novel
    meaning: "\uC18C\uC124"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The cook says this would make a bad novel.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uB9AC\uC0AC\uB294 \uC774\uAC8C \uD615\uD3B8\uC5C6\uB294
      \uC18C\uC124\uC774 \uB420 \uAC70\uB77C \uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 194
    storyHeading: 
  - id: nuclear
    english: nuclear
    meaning: "\uD575\uC758, \uC6D0\uC790\uB825\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The country closed its last nuclear plant.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uB098\uB77C\uB294 \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uC6D0\uC790\uB825
      \uBC1C\uC804\uC18C\uB97C \uB2EB\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: numerous
    english: numerous
    meaning: "\uC218\uB9CE\uC740"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Numerous bites cover my arms.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC218\uB9CE\uC740 \uBB3C\uB9B0 \uC790\uAD6D\uC774 \uD314\uC744
      \uB36E\uACE0 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 255
    storyHeading: 
  - id: obtain
    english: obtain
    meaning: "\uC190\uC5D0 \uB123\uB2E4, \uC5BB\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I managed to obtain one onion by trading my hat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBAA8\uC790\uB97C \uC8FC\uACE0 \uC591\uD30C \uD558\uB098\uB97C
      \uC190\uC5D0 \uB123\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 163
    storyHeading: 
  - id: origin
    english: origin
    meaning: "\uAE30\uC6D0, \uCD9C\uC2E0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: It said the origin of all words lies under the desert.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBAA8\uB4E0 \uB9D0\uC758 \uAE30\uC6D0\uC740 \uC0AC\uB9C9 \uC544\uB798\uC5D0
      \uC788\uB2E4\uACE0 \uC4F0\uC5EC \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 287
    storyHeading: 
  - id: outcome
    english: outcome
    meaning: "\uACB0\uACFC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The outcome of that plan is written on my face.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uACC4\uD68D\uC758 \uACB0\uACFC\uB294 \uB0B4 \uC5BC\uAD74\uC5D0
      \uC801\uD600 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 245
    storyHeading: 
  - id: output
    english: output
    meaning: "\uC0DD\uC0B0\uB7C9, \uCD9C\uB825"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My daily output is three lines and one tear.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uD558\uB8E8 \uC0DD\uC0B0\uB7C9\uC740 \uC138 \uC904\uACFC
      \uB208\uBB3C \uD55C \uBC29\uC6B8\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 252
    storyHeading: 
  - id: outstanding
    english: outstanding
    meaning: "\uB6F0\uC5B4\uB09C, (\uB3C8\uC774) \uBBF8\uC9C0\uBD88\uB41C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The mule showed outstanding courage by running away.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uB3C4\uB9DD\uCE58\uB294 \uAC83\uC73C\uB85C
      \uB6F0\uC5B4\uB09C \uC6A9\uAE30\uB97C \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 205
    storyHeading: 
  - id: overcome
    english: overcome
    meaning: "\uADF9\uBCF5\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: We overcome one swamp, and another one appears.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2AA \uD558\uB098\uB97C \uADF9\uBCF5\uD558\uBA74 \uB610 \uD558\uB098\uAC00
      \uB098\uD0C0\uB09C\uB2E4."
    story: 94
    storyHeading: 
  - id: pace
    english: pace
    meaning: "\uC18D\uB3C4, \uBCF4\uD3ED"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I count each pace, and then I forget the number.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC78\uC74C\uC744 \uC138\uB2E4\uAC00 \uC22B\uC790\uB97C \uC78A\uC5B4\uBC84\uB9B0\uB2E4."
    story: 49
    storyHeading: 
  - id: panel
    english: panel
    meaning: "\uD310, \uC704\uC6D0\uB2E8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A stone panel slid open when I said the right word.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9DE\uB294 \uB9D0\uC744 \uD558\uC790 \uB3CC\uD310\uC774 \uBC00\uB824
      \uC5F4\uB838\uB2E4."
    story: 231
    storyHeading: 
  - id: parallel
    english: parallel
    meaning: "\uD3C9\uD589\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Two parallel rivers ran on either side of us.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD3C9\uD589\uD55C \uB450 \uAC15\uC774 \uC6B0\uB9AC \uC591\uC606\uC73C\uB85C
      \uD758\uB800\uB2E4."
    story: 122
    storyHeading: 
  - id: parliament
    english: parliament
    meaning: "\uC758\uD68C, \uAD6D\uD68C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Our parliament meets whenever the mule wakes up.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC \uC758\uD68C\uB294 \uB178\uC0C8\uAC00 \uAE70 \uB54C\uB9C8\uB2E4
      \uC5F4\uB9B0\uB2E4."
    story: 152
    storyHeading: 
  - id: participate
    english: participate
    meaning: "\uCC38\uAC00\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: The mule refuses to participate in the dark.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC5B4\uB460 \uC18D \uC77C\uC5D0 \uCC38\uAC00\uD558\uAE30\uB97C
      \uAC70\uBD80\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 273
    storyHeading: 
  - id: passage
    english: passage
    meaning: "\uD1B5\uB85C, \uAD6C\uC808"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A narrow passage led down under the river.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC881\uC740 \uD1B5\uB85C\uAC00 \uAC15 \uC544\uB798\uB85C \uC774\uC5B4\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 268
    storyHeading: "\uB3D9\uAD74"
  - id: passenger
    english: passenger
    meaning: "\uC2B9\uAC1D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule is a terrible passenger.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uB054\uCC0D\uD55C \uC2B9\uAC1D\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 120
    storyHeading: 
  - id: passion
    english: passion
    meaning: "\uC5F4\uC815"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My passion for maps has dried up; my socks have not.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C0\uB3C4\uB97C \uD5A5\uD55C \uC5F4\uC815\uC740 \uB9D0\uB790\uC9C0\uB9CC
      \uC591\uB9D0\uC740 \uC548 \uB9D0\uB790\uB2E4."
    story: 136
    storyHeading: 
  - id: peak
    english: peak
    meaning: "\uC815\uC0C1, \uC808\uC815"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: From the peak of a tree, I saw stone roofs.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uBB34 \uAF2D\uB300\uAE30\uC5D0\uC11C \uB3CC\uC9C0\uBD95\uB4E4\uC774
      \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 173
    storyHeading: "70\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: penalty
    english: penalty
    meaning: "\uBC8C\uCE59, \uCC98\uBC8C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The penalty was washing the pot for a week.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC8C\uCE59\uC740 \uC77C\uC8FC\uC77C \uB3D9\uC548 \uB0C4\uBE44
      \uB2E6\uAE30\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 81
    storyHeading: 
  - id: permanent
    english: permanent
    meaning: "\uC601\uAD6C\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The mud on my coat is permanent.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC678\uD22C\uC758 \uC9C4\uD759\uC740 \uC601\uAD6C\uC801\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 225
    storyHeading: 
  - id: perspective
    english: perspective
    meaning: "\uAD00\uC810, \uC2DC\uAC01"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: From a mule's perspective, this is all my fault.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uC758 \uAD00\uC810\uC5D0\uC11C \uBCF4\uBA74 \uC804\uBD80
      \uB0B4 \uC798\uBABB\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 199
    storyHeading: 
  - id: phase
    english: phase
    meaning: "\uB2E8\uACC4, \uAD6D\uBA74"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The last phase of this trip is going down.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC5EC\uD589\uC758 \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uB2E8\uACC4\uB294
      \uB0B4\uB824\uAC00\uB294 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 288
    storyHeading: 
  - id: philosophy
    english: philosophy
    meaning: "\uCCA0\uD559"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'My philosophy now: keep your socks dry.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uB0B4 \uCCA0\uD559\uC740 \uC591\uB9D0\uC744 \uB9C8\uB974\uAC8C
      \uB450\uB294 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 198
    storyHeading: 
  - id: phrase
    english: phrase
    meaning: "\uAD6C, \uC5B4\uAD6C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: One phrase kept changing each time I read it.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C \uAD6C\uC808\uC740 \uC77D\uC744 \uB54C\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uBC14\uB00C\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 70
    storyHeading: 
  - id: physics
    english: physics
    meaning: "\uBB3C\uB9AC\uD559"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Physics says mules cannot climb that. It climbed.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBB3C\uB9AC\uD559\uC0C1 \uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC800\uAE38 \uBABB
      \uC624\uB978\uB2E4. \uC62C\uB790\uB2E4."
    story: 172
    storyHeading: 
  - id: platform
    english: platform
    meaning: "\uC2B9\uAC15\uC7A5, \uB2E8\uC0C1"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We rested on a stone platform above the water.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBB3C \uC704 \uB3CC \uB2E8\uC0C1\uC5D0\uC11C \uC26C\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 232
    storyHeading: 
  - id: plot
    english: plot
    meaning: "\uC904\uAC70\uB9AC, \uC74C\uBAA8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I suspect a plot, possibly by the raccoon.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC74C\uBAA8\uAC00 \uC788\uB294 \uAC83 \uAC19\uB2E4. \uC544\uB9C8
      \uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC\uAC00 \uAFB8\uBBFC."
    story: 57
    storyHeading: 
  - id: polish
    english: polish
    meaning: "\uB2E6\uB2E4, \uC724\uC744 \uB0B4\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I still polish my badge every morning.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uB294 \uC544\uC9C1\uB3C4 \uC544\uCE68\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uBC30\uC9C0\uB97C
      \uB2E6\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 96
    storyHeading: 
  - id: poll
    english: poll
    meaning: "\uC5EC\uB860 \uC870\uC0AC, \uD22C\uD45C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We held a poll. North won, but nobody knew where it was.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uB860 \uC870\uC0AC\uB97C \uD588\uB2E4. \uBD81\uCABD\uC774
      \uC774\uACBC\uC9C0\uB9CC \uC5B4\uB518\uC9C0\uB294 \uBAB0\uB790\uB2E4."
    story: 23
    storyHeading: 
  - id: portion
    english: portion
    meaning: "\uBD80\uBD84, 1\uC778\uBD84"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule ate my portion.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uAC00 \uB0B4 \uBAAB\uC744 \uBA39\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 26
    storyHeading: 
  - id: praise
    english: praise
    meaning: "\uCE6D\uCC2C\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: Someone should praise the mule. Not me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB204\uAD70\uAC00\uB294 \uB178\uC0C8\uB97C \uCE6D\uCC2C\uD574\uC57C
      \uD55C\uB2E4. \uB098\uB294 \uC544\uB2C8\uACE0."
    story: 170
    storyHeading: 
  - id: premium
    english: premium
    meaning: "\uBCF4\uD5D8\uB8CC, \uD560\uC99D\uAE08"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The ministry refused to pay my insurance premium.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uB294 \uB0B4 \uBCF4\uD5D8\uB8CC\uB97C \uB0B4\uC8FC\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 8
    storyHeading: 
  - id: priest
    english: priest
    meaning: "\uC131\uC9C1\uC790, \uC0AC\uC81C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A stone priest held a blank tablet.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB3CC\uB85C \uB41C \uC0AC\uC81C\uAC00 \uBE48 \uC11D\uD310\uC744
      \uB4E4\uACE0 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 177
    storyHeading: 
  - id: principal
    english: principal
    meaning: "\uAD50\uC7A5, \uC6B0\uB450\uBA38\uB9AC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My old principal would have hated this trip.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC61B \uAD50\uC7A5 \uC120\uC0DD\uB2D8\uC774\uB77C\uBA74 \uC774
      \uC5EC\uD589\uC744 \uC2EB\uC5B4\uD588\uC744 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 191
    storyHeading: 
  - id: prior
    english: prior
    meaning: "\uC774\uC804\uC758, \uC6B0\uC120\uD558\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Prior maps show nothing here at all.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC804 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uB4E4\uC5D0\uB294 \uC5EC\uAE30\uC5D0
      \uC544\uBB34\uAC83\uB3C4 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 235
    storyHeading: 
  - id: professor
    english: professor
    meaning: "\uAD50\uC218"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A professor once told me the jungle was a myth.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C \uAD50\uC218\uB294 \uC815\uAE00\uC774 \uC2E0\uD654\uB77C\uACE0
      \uB9D0\uD588\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 217
    storyHeading: 
  - id: profile
    english: profile
    meaning: "\uC606\uBAA8\uC2B5, \uD504\uB85C\uD544"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: In profile, one looked just like my old teacher.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8\uC911 \uD558\uB098\uB294 \uC606\uBAA8\uC2B5\uC774 \uAF2D
      \uC61B \uC120\uC0DD\uB2D8 \uAC19\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 190
    storyHeading: 
  - id: prominent
    english: prominent
    meaning: "\uC800\uBA85\uD55C, \uB208\uC5D0 \uB744\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: A prominent rock stood in the middle of the river.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC15 \uD55C\uAC00\uC6B4\uB370 \uB208\uC5D0 \uB744\uB294 \uBC14\uC704\uAC00
      \uC11C \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 215
    storyHeading: 
  - id: proof
    english: proof
    meaning: "\uC99D\uAC70, \uC99D\uBA85"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My muddy boots are my only proof I walked at all.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C4\uD759 \uBB3B\uC740 \uC7A5\uD654\uB9CC\uC774 \uB0B4\uAC00
      \uAC78\uC5C8\uB2E4\uB294 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uC99D\uAC70\uB2E4."
    story: 73
    storyHeading: 
  - id: protein
    english: protein
    meaning: "\uB2E8\uBC31\uC9C8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Bugs are good protein, I tell myself.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC8C\uB808\uB294 \uC88B\uC740 \uB2E8\uBC31\uC9C8\uC774\uB77C\uACE0
      \uC2A4\uC2A4\uB85C\uC5D0\uAC8C \uB9D0\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 251
    storyHeading: 
  - id: province
    english: province
    meaning: "\uC9C0\uBC29, \uB3C4(\u9053)"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: No province claims this forest.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5B4\uB290 \uC9C0\uBC29\uB3C4 \uC774 \uC232\uC744 \uC790\uAE30
      \uB545\uC774\uB77C \uD558\uC9C0 \uC54A\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 109
    storyHeading: 
  - id: psychology
    english: psychology
    meaning: "\uC2EC\uB9AC\uD559"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I studied mule psychology. It is mostly spite.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8 \uC2EC\uB9AC\uD559\uC744 \uC5F0\uAD6C\uD588\uB2E4.
      \uB300\uBD80\uBD84 \uC559\uC2EC\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 171
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rally
    english: rally
    meaning: "\uC9D1\uD68C, \uB300\uD68C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Nobody came to my rally except the mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC9D1\uD68C\uC5D0\uB294 \uB178\uC0C8 \uB9D0\uACE0 \uC544\uBB34\uB3C4
      \uC624\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 145
    storyHeading: 
  - id: random
    english: random
    meaning: "\uBB34\uC791\uC704\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Every signpost pointed in a random direction.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC815\uD45C\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uBB34\uC791\uC704 \uBC29\uD5A5\uC744
      \uAC00\uB9AC\uCF30\uB2E4."
    story: 19
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rank
    english: rank
    meaning: "\uACC4\uAE09, \uC21C\uC704"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: In this jungle, my rank means nothing.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC815\uAE00\uC5D0\uC11C \uB0B4 \uACC4\uAE09\uC740 \uC544\uBB34
      \uC758\uBBF8\uB3C4 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 210
    storyHeading: 
  - id: raw
    english: raw
    meaning: "\uB0A0\uAC83\uC758, \uAC00\uACF5\uD558\uC9C0 \uC54A\uC740"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Breakfast was a raw mushroom, which was a mistake.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC544\uCE68\uC740 \uB0A0 \uBC84\uC12F\uC774\uC5C8\uACE0, \uADF8\uAC74
      \uC2E4\uC218\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 35
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rear
    english: rear
    meaning: "\uB4A4\uCABD, \uD6C4\uBC29"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Something moved at the rear of the boat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC30 \uB4A4\uCABD\uC5D0\uC11C \uBB54\uAC00 \uC6C0\uC9C1\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 124
    storyHeading: 
  - id: reform
    english: reform
    meaning: "\uAC1C\uD601"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'My first reform: more soup.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uCCAB \uAC1C\uD601\uC740 \uC218\uD504\uB97C \uB298\uB9AC\uB294
      \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 146
    storyHeading: 
  - id: regret
    english: regret
    meaning: "\uD6C4\uD68C\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I regret nothing but the biscuits.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBE44\uC2A4\uD0B7 \uB9D0\uACE0\uB294 \uD6C4\uD68C\uD558\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 92
    storyHeading: 
  - id: relevant
    english: relevant
    meaning: "\uAD00\uB828 \uC788\uB294, \uC801\uC808\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: None of my training seems relevant now.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4\uAC00 \uBC1B\uC740 \uD6C8\uB828\uC740 \uC774\uC81C \uC544\uBB34
      \uAD00\uB828\uC774 \uC5C6\uC5B4 \uBCF4\uC778\uB2E4."
    story: 222
    storyHeading: 
  - id: religion
    english: religion
    meaning: "\uC885\uAD50"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I do not know this religion, but I know its fear.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC885\uAD50\uB294 \uBAA8\uB974\uC9C0\uB9CC, \uADF8 \uB450\uB824\uC6C0\uC740
      \uC548\uB2E4."
    story: 179
    storyHeading: 
  - id: remote
    english: remote
    meaning: "\uBA3C, \uC678\uB534"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: This is the most remote place on my map.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uAC00 \uB0B4 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC5D0\uC11C \uAC00\uC7A5
      \uC678\uB534 \uACF3\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 104
    storyHeading: 
  - id: republic
    english: republic
    meaning: "\uACF5\uD654\uAD6D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We are now a republic of one person and one mule.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC\uB294 \uC774\uC81C \uC0AC\uB78C \uD558\uB098\uC640
      \uB178\uC0C8 \uD558\uB098\uC758 \uACF5\uD654\uAD6D\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 151
    storyHeading: 
  - id: reputation
    english: reputation
    meaning: "\uD3C9\uD310, \uBA85\uC131"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My reputation at the ministry is surely ruined.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uC5D0\uC11C \uB0B4 \uD3C9\uD310\uC740 \uD2C0\uB9BC\uC5C6\uC774
      \uB05D\uC7A5\uB0AC\uB2E4."
    story: 212
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rescue
    english: rescue
    meaning: "\uAD6C\uC870\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: Nobody will rescue us, so I will keep walking.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC544\uBB34\uB3C4 \uC6B0\uB9AC\uB97C \uAD6C\uC870\uD558\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uC744 \uD14C\uB2C8 \uACC4\uC18D \uAC77\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 112
    storyHeading: 
  - id: resort
    english: resort
    meaning: "\uD734\uC591\uC9C0, \uC758\uC9C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The ministry called this place a resort on the form.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBD80\uCC98\uB294 \uC11C\uB958\uC5D0 \uC774\uACF3\uC744 \uD734\uC591\uC9C0\uB77C\uACE0
      \uC801\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 134
    storyHeading: 
  - id: retail
    english: retail
    meaning: "\uC18C\uB9E4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I miss retail shops and their dry socks.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uB978 \uC591\uB9D0\uC744 \uD30C\uB294 \uC18C\uB9E4\uC810\uB4E4\uC774
      \uADF8\uB9BD\uB2E4."
    story: 160
    storyHeading: 
  - id: reveal
    english: reveal
    meaning: "\uB4DC\uB7EC\uB0B4\uB2E4, \uBC1D\uD788\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: The light began to reveal writing on the walls.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBE5B\uC774 \uBCBD\uC758 \uAE00\uC528\uB97C \uB4DC\uB7EC\uB0B4\uAE30
      \uC2DC\uC791\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 286
    storyHeading: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uCABD"
  - id: revenge
    english: revenge
    meaning: "\uBCF5\uC218"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My revenge on the raccoon will be long and polite.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC\uC5D0 \uB300\uD55C \uB0B4 \uBCF5\uC218\uB294
      \uAE38\uACE0 \uC815\uC911\uD560 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 93
    storyHeading: 
  - id: reverse
    english: reverse
    meaning: "\uB4A4\uBC14\uAFB8\uB2E4, \uAC70\uAFB8\uB85C \uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: It would reverse every sign and laugh at me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB294 \uD45C\uC9C0\uD310\uC744 \uAC70\uAFB8\uB85C
      \uB3CC\uB824\uB193\uACE0 \uB098\uB97C \uBE44\uC6C3\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 55
    storyHeading: 
  - id: revolution
    english: revolution
    meaning: "\uD601\uBA85"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule started a revolution and took the soup.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uAC00 \uD601\uBA85\uC744 \uC77C\uC73C\uCF1C \uC218\uD504\uB97C
      \uAC00\uC838\uAC14\uB2E4."
    story: 147
    storyHeading: 
  - id: reward
    english: reward
    meaning: "\uBCF4\uC0C1, \uBCF4\uB2F5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The mule goes back with this journal and an apple as reward.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC774 \uC77C\uC9C0\uC640 \uBCF4\uC0C1\uC73C\uB85C
      \uBC1B\uC740 \uC0AC\uACFC\uB97C \uB4E4\uACE0 \uB3CC\uC544\uAC04\uB2E4."
    story: 289
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rid
    english: rid
    meaning: "\uC5C6\uC560\uB2E4, \uC81C\uAC70\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I cannot get rid of this mud.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC9C4\uD759\uC744 \uC5C6\uC568 \uC218\uAC00 \uC5C6\uB2E4."
    story: 95
    storyHeading: 
  - id: romantic
    english: romantic
    meaning: "\uB0AD\uB9CC\uC801\uC778"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I once thought travel was romantic.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C\uB54C \uC5EC\uD589\uC774 \uB0AD\uB9CC\uC801\uC774\uB77C
      \uC0DD\uAC01\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 135
    storyHeading: 
  - id: routine
    english: routine
    meaning: "\uC77C\uACFC, \uD310\uC5D0 \uBC15\uD78C \uC77C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'My routine is simple: walk, eat, write, get lost.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC77C\uACFC\uB294 \uAC04\uB2E8\uD558\uB2E4. \uAC77\uACE0,
      \uBA39\uACE0, \uC4F0\uACE0, \uAE38\uC744 \uC783\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 48
    storyHeading: 
  - id: rural
    english: rural
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uACE8\uC758, \uB18D\uCD0C\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: We left the last rural farm behind at noon.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC815\uC624\uC5D0 \uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uC2DC\uACE8 \uB18D\uC7A5\uC744
      \uC9C0\uB098\uCCE4\uB2E4."
    story: 14
    storyHeading: 
  - id: sacrifice
    english: sacrifice
    meaning: "\uD76C\uC0DD"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Keeping one word takes the sacrifice of another.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9D0 \uD558\uB098\uB97C \uC9C0\uD0A4\uB824\uBA74 \uB2E4\uB978
      \uB9D0\uC744 \uD76C\uC0DD\uD574\uC57C \uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 284
    storyHeading: 
  - id: satellite
    english: satellite
    meaning: "\uC704\uC131"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A satellite sends pictures of the Earth.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC704\uC131\uC740 \uC9C0\uAD6C \uC0AC\uC9C4\uC744 \uBCF4\uB0B8\uB2E4."
    story: 0
    storyHeading: 
  - id: scheme
    english: scheme
    meaning: "\uACC4\uD68D, \uCC45\uB7B5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Its whole scheme was to keep us walking in circles.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uB9AC\uB97C \uACC4\uC18D \uB9F4\uB3CC\uAC8C \uD558\uB294
      \uAC83\uC774 \uADF8 \uACC4\uB7B5\uC758 \uC804\uBD80\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 56
    storyHeading: 
  - id: sector
    english: sector
    meaning: "\uBD80\uBB38, \uBD84\uC57C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: This sector of the map is only a coffee stain.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C0\uB3C4\uC758 \uC774 \uBD80\uBD84\uC740 \uCEE4\uD53C \uC5BC\uB8E9\uBFD0\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 265
    storyHeading: 
  - id: sequence
    english: sequence
    meaning: "\uC21C\uC11C, \uC5F0\uC18D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I number the trees in sequence to find my way.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAE38\uC744 \uCC3E\uC73C\uB824\uACE0 \uB098\uBB34\uC5D0 \uC21C\uC11C\uB300\uB85C
      \uBC88\uD638\uB97C \uB9E4\uAE34\uB2E4."
    story: 47
    storyHeading: 
  - id: severe
    english: severe
    meaning: "\uC2EC\uAC01\uD55C, \uC5C4\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The cold was severe, and the mule sat on me.
    exampleMeaning: "\uCD94\uC704\uAC00 \uC2EC\uD588\uACE0, \uB178\uC0C8\uAC00 \uB0B4
      \uC704\uC5D0 \uC549\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 76
    storyHeading: 
  - id: silent
    english: silent
    meaning: "\uC870\uC6A9\uD55C, \uB9D0\uC774 \uC5C6\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The forest went silent when the words left.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9D0\uC774 \uB5A0\uB098\uC790 \uC232\uC774 \uC870\uC6A9\uD574\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 79
    storyHeading: 
  - id: sin
    english: sin
    meaning: "\uC8C4, \uC8C4\uC545"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: My only sin was signing that form.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uC8C4\uB294 \uADF8 \uC11C\uB958\uC5D0
      \uC11C\uBA85\uD55C \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 89
    storyHeading: 
  - id: sole
    english: sole
    meaning: "\uC720\uC77C\uD55C, \uB2E8\uB3C5\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My sole light is one small stone.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uBE5B\uC740 \uC791\uC740 \uB3CC \uD558\uB098\uB2E4."
    story: 276
    storyHeading: 
  - id: split
    english: split
    meaning: "\uCABC\uAC1C\uB2E4, \uB098\uB204\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: We decided to split the last biscuit into six.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB9C8\uC9C0\uB9C9 \uBE44\uC2A4\uD0B7\uC744 \uC5EC\uC12F\uC73C\uB85C
      \uCABC\uAC1C\uAE30\uB85C \uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 25
    storyHeading: 
  - id: status
    english: status
    meaning: "\uC9C0\uC704, \uC0C1\uD0DC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'My status: alive, lost, slightly purple.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC0C1\uD0DC: \uC0B4\uC544 \uC788\uC74C, \uAE38 \uC783\uC74C,
      \uC57D\uAC04 \uBCF4\uB77C\uC0C9."
    story: 211
    storyHeading: 
  - id: stem
    english: stem
    meaning: "\uC904\uAE30"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I chewed a stem and saw three moons for an hour.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC904\uAE30\uB97C \uC539\uC5C8\uB354\uB2C8 \uD55C \uC2DC\uAC04
      \uB3D9\uC548 \uB2EC\uC774 \uC138 \uAC1C\uB85C \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 36
    storyHeading: 
  - id: strip
    english: strip
    meaning: "\uAE38\uACE0 \uAC00\uB290\uB2E4\uB780 \uC870\uAC01"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A strip of cloth now marks each tree I pass.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uC9C0\uB098\uB294 \uB098\uBB34\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uCC9C
      \uC870\uAC01\uC744 \uBB36\uC5B4 \uD45C\uC2DC\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 45
    storyHeading: 
  - id: stroke
    english: stroke
    meaning: "\uD0C0\uACA9, \uB1CC\uC878\uC911"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: With one stroke, I cut the vine.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD55C \uBC88\uC758 \uD0C0\uACA9\uC73C\uB85C \uB369\uAD74\uC744
      \uC798\uB790\uB2E4."
    story: 262
    storyHeading: 
  - id: submit
    english: submit
    meaning: "\uC81C\uCD9C\uD558\uB2E4, \uAD74\uBCF5\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I will submit it when I find a way home.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9D1\uC5D0 \uAC08 \uAE38\uC744 \uCC3E\uC73C\uBA74 \uC81C\uCD9C\uD560
      \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 30
    storyHeading: 
  - id: suck
    english: suck
    meaning: "\uBE68\uB2E4, \uBE68\uC544\uB4E4\uC774\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: Leeches suck blood, so I keep my boots on.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC70\uBA38\uB9AC\uAC00 \uD53C\uB97C \uBE68\uAE30 \uB54C\uBB38\uC5D0
      \uC7A5\uD654\uB97C \uBC97\uC9C0 \uC54A\uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 87
    storyHeading: 
  - id: summit
    english: summit
    meaning: "\uC815\uC0C1, \uC815\uC0C1 \uD68C\uB2F4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: From the summit of the rock, I saw more rock.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBC14\uC704 \uC815\uC0C1\uC5D0 \uC62C\uB790\uB354\uB2C8 \uB354
      \uB9CE\uC740 \uBC14\uC704\uAC00 \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4."
    story: 216
    storyHeading: 
  - id: superior
    english: superior
    meaning: "\uC6B0\uC218\uD55C, \uC0C1\uAE09\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: A superior officer would still ask for the form.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC0C1\uAE09 \uAD00\uB9AC\uB77C\uBA74 \uADF8\uB798\uB3C4 \uC11C\uB958\uB97C
      \uB2EC\uB77C\uACE0 \uD560 \uAC83\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 209
    storyHeading: 
  - id: surgery
    english: surgery
    meaning: "\uC218\uC220"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I did surgery on my boot with a fork.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD3EC\uD06C\uB85C \uC7A5\uD654\uB97C \uC218\uC220\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 258
    storyHeading: 
  - id: swear
    english: swear
    meaning: "\uB9F9\uC138\uD558\uB2E4, \uC695\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: I swear I will never complain about office work again.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB2E4\uC2DC\uB294 \uC0AC\uBB34 \uC77C\uB85C \uBD88\uD3C9\uD558\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uACA0\uB2E4\uACE0 \uB9F9\uC138\uD55C\uB2E4."
    story: 88
    storyHeading: 
  - id: symbol
    english: symbol
    meaning: "\uC0C1\uC9D5, \uAE30\uD638"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Every symbol on the wall had a hole in the middle.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBCBD\uC758 \uC0C1\uC9D5\uB9C8\uB2E4 \uAC00\uC6B4\uB370\uC5D0
      \uAD6C\uBA4D\uC774 \uB098 \uC788\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 180
    storyHeading: 
  - id: tag
    english: tag
    meaning: "\uAF2C\uB9AC\uD45C, \uD0DC\uADF8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'I put a tag on the mule: do not eat my report.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB178\uC0C8\uC5D0 \uAF2C\uB9AC\uD45C\uB97C \uB2EC\uC558\uB2E4.
      \uB0B4 \uBCF4\uACE0\uC11C\uB97C \uBA39\uC9C0 \uB9D0 \uAC83."
    story: 46
    storyHeading: 
  - id: talent
    english: talent
    meaning: "\uC7AC\uB2A5"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Running away is my greatest talent.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB3C4\uB9DD\uCE58\uAE30\uB294 \uB0B4 \uAC00\uC7A5 \uD070 \uC7AC\uB2A5\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 206
    storyHeading: 
  - id: task
    english: task
    meaning: "\uACFC\uC81C, \uC5C5\uBB34"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'My task sounded simple: fix the signposts.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uC5C5\uBB34\uB294 \uAC04\uB2E8\uD574 \uBCF4\uC600\uB2E4.
      \uC774\uC815\uD45C\uB97C \uACE0\uCE60 \uAC83."
    story: 5
    storyHeading: 
  - id: temple
    english: temple
    meaning: "\uC0AC\uC6D0, \uC808"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The temple had no door and no name.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC0AC\uC6D0\uC5D0\uB294 \uBB38\uB3C4 \uC774\uB984\uB3C4 \uC5C6\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 175
    storyHeading: 
  - id: temporary
    english: temporary
    meaning: "\uC77C\uC2DC\uC801\uC778, \uC784\uC2DC\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: This camp is temporary, like everything else.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC57C\uC601\uC9C0\uB294 \uB2E4\uB978 \uBAA8\uB4E0 \uAC83\uCC98\uB7FC
      \uC784\uC2DC\uB2E4."
    story: 224
    storyHeading: 
  - id: terminal
    english: terminal
    meaning: "\uD130\uBBF8\uB110, \uC885\uC810"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The river's terminal point was a hole in the ground.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC15\uC758 \uC885\uC810\uC740 \uB545\uC5D0 \uB09C \uAD6C\uBA4D\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 267
    storyHeading: 
  - id: territory
    english: territory
    meaning: "\uC601\uD1A0, \uC601\uC5ED"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We are deep in raccoon territory now.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774\uC81C \uC6B0\uB9AC\uB294 \uB108\uAD6C\uB9AC \uC601\uD1A0
      \uAE4A\uC219\uC774 \uC788\uB2E4."
    story: 107
    storyHeading: 
  - id: theme
    english: theme
    meaning: "\uC8FC\uC81C, \uD14C\uB9C8"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The theme of this journal is mud.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC774 \uC77C\uC9C0\uC758 \uC8FC\uC81C\uB294 \uC9C4\uD759\uC774\uB2E4."
    story: 197
    storyHeading: 
  - id: therapy
    english: therapy
    meaning: "\uCE58\uB8CC, \uC694\uBC95"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Writing here is my only therapy.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uC5D0 \uC4F0\uB294 \uAC83\uC774 \uB0B4 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C
      \uCE58\uB8CC\uB2E4."
    story: 259
    storyHeading: 
  - id: thread
    english: thread
    meaning: "\uC2E4, (\uC774\uC57C\uAE30\uC758) \uB9E5\uB77D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I sewed my coat with thread from my hat.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBAA8\uC790\uC5D0\uC11C \uBE80 \uC2E4\uB85C \uC678\uD22C\uB97C
      \uAFF0\uB9F8\uB2E4."
    story: 44
    storyHeading: 
  - id: tissue
    english: tissue
    meaning: "(\uC0DD\uBB3C\uC758) \uC870\uC9C1, \uD654\uC7A5\uC9C0"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: I would trade a map for one tissue.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C0\uB3C4 \uD55C \uC7A5\uC744 \uD654\uC7A5\uC9C0 \uD558\uB098\uC640
      \uBC14\uAFB8\uACA0\uB2E4."
    story: 257
    storyHeading: 
  - id: trail
    english: trail
    meaning: "\uC624\uC194\uAE38, \uC790\uAD6D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The trail into the forest was wide and friendly.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC232\uC73C\uB85C \uB4DC\uB294 \uC624\uC194\uAE38\uC740 \uB113\uACE0
      \uB2E4\uC815\uD588\uB2E4."
    story: 12
    storyHeading: "1\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: transition
    english: transition
    meaning: "\uC804\uD658, \uBCC0\uCC9C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The transition from forest to jungle was sudden.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC232\uC5D0\uC11C \uC815\uAE00\uB85C\uC758 \uC804\uD658\uC740
      \uAC11\uC791\uC2A4\uB7EC\uC6E0\uB2E4."
    story: 116
    storyHeading: "41\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: treaty
    english: treaty
    meaning: "\uC870\uC57D"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'We signed a treaty: I carry the bags, it carries nothing.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC870\uC57D\uC744 \uB9FA\uC5C8\uB2E4. \uAC00\uBC29\uC740 \uB0B4\uAC00
      \uB4E4\uACE0, \uB178\uC0C8\uB294 \uC548 \uB4E0\uB2E4."
    story: 148
    storyHeading: 
  - id: trend
    english: trend
    meaning: "\uACBD\uD5A5, \uCD94\uC138"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: 'The current trend: fewer words, more frogs.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC694\uC998 \uCD94\uC138\uB294 \uB9D0\uC740 \uC904\uACE0 \uAC1C\uAD6C\uB9AC\uB294
      \uB294\uB2E4."
    story: 247
    storyHeading: 
  - id: tube
    english: tube
    meaning: "\uAD00, \uD29C\uBE0C"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: A hollow reed makes a good tube for drinking.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC18D \uBE48 \uAC08\uB300\uB294 \uB9C8\uC2DC\uAE30 \uC88B\uC740
      \uAD00\uC774 \uB41C\uB2E4."
    story: 40
    storyHeading: 
  - id: ultimate
    english: ultimate
    meaning: "\uAD81\uADF9\uC801\uC778, \uCD5C\uD6C4\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: The ultimate goal is now one dry night.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAD81\uADF9\uC801\uC778 \uBAA9\uD45C\uB294 \uC774\uC81C \uB9C8\uB978
      \uBC24 \uD558\uB098\uB2E4."
    story: 244
    storyHeading: 
  - id: unique
    english: unique
    meaning: "\uB3C5\uD2B9\uD55C, \uC720\uC77C\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Its unique skill was copying everything I said, badly.
    exampleMeaning: "\uADF8 \uB3C5\uD2B9\uD55C \uC7AC\uC8FC\uB294 \uB0B4 \uB9D0\uC744
      \uC5C9\uD130\uB9AC\uB85C \uB530\uB77C \uD558\uB294 \uAC83\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 125
    storyHeading: 
  - id: universe
    english: universe
    meaning: "\uC6B0\uC8FC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: The universe owes me one dry sock.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC6B0\uC8FC\uB294 \uB0B4\uAC8C \uB9C8\uB978 \uC591\uB9D0 \uD558\uB098\uB97C
      \uBE5A\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 240
    storyHeading: 
  - id: update
    english: update
    meaning: "\uAC31\uC2E0\uD558\uB2E4, \uC5C5\uB370\uC774\uD2B8\uD558\uB2E4"
    partOfSpeech: v
    example: 'Let me update the map: we are lost.'
    exampleMeaning: "\uC9C0\uB3C4\uB97C \uAC31\uC2E0\uD55C\uB2E4. \uC6B0\uB9AC\uB294
      \uAE38\uC744 \uC783\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 53
    storyHeading: 
  - id: urban
    english: urban
    meaning: "\uB3C4\uC2DC\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: I was an urban person once, with shoes.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB098\uB294 \uD55C\uB54C \uC2E0\uBC1C \uC2E0\uC740 \uB3C4\uC2DC
      \uC0AC\uB78C\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 238
    storyHeading: 
  - id: valid
    english: valid
    meaning: "\uC720\uD6A8\uD55C, \uD0C0\uB2F9\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: My travel pass is no longer valid.
    exampleMeaning: "\uB0B4 \uD1B5\uD589\uC99D\uC740 \uB354 \uC774\uC0C1 \uC720\uD6A8\uD558\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uB2E4."
    story: 223
    storyHeading: 
  - id: vast
    english: vast
    meaning: "\uAD11\uB300\uD55C, \uB9C9\uB300\uD55C"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: It is more vast than any map can hold.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5B4\uB5A4 \uC9C0\uB3C4\uC5D0 \uB2F4\uAE30\uC5D0\uB3C4 \uB108\uBB34
      \uAD11\uB300\uD558\uB2E4."
    story: 106
    storyHeading: 
  - id: vessel
    english: vessel
    meaning: "\uC120\uBC15, \uC6A9\uAE30, \uD608\uAD00"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: We built a vessel out of logs and bad ideas.
    exampleMeaning: "\uD1B5\uB098\uBB34\uC640 \uB098\uC05C \uC0DD\uAC01\uB4E4\uB85C
      \uBC30\uB97C \uB9CC\uB4E4\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 118
    storyHeading: 
  - id: vice
    english: vice
    meaning: "\uC545, \uBD80\uB3C4\uB355"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Biscuits were my one vice.
    exampleMeaning: "\uBE44\uC2A4\uD0B7\uC740 \uB098\uC758 \uC720\uC77C\uD55C \uC545\uC774\uC5C8\uB2E4."
    story: 90
    storyHeading: 
  - id: victory
    english: victory
    meaning: "\uC2B9\uB9AC"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Finding the river felt like victory.
    exampleMeaning: "\uAC15\uC744 \uCC3E\uC740 \uAC83\uC740 \uC2B9\uB9AC\uCC98\uB7FC
      \uB290\uAEF4\uC84C\uB2E4."
    story: 113
    storyHeading: 
  - id: virus
    english: virus
    meaning: "\uBC14\uC774\uB7EC\uC2A4"
    partOfSpeech: n
    example: Forgetting spreads like a virus here.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C\uB294 \uC78A\uC74C\uC774 \uBC14\uC774\uB7EC\uC2A4\uCC98\uB7FC
      \uD37C\uC9C4\uB2E4."
    story: 260
    storyHeading: 
  - id: visible
    english: visible
    meaning: "\uB208\uC5D0 \uBCF4\uC774\uB294"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: By evening, the road was no longer visible.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC800\uB141\uC774 \uB418\uC790 \uAE38\uC774 \uB354\uB294 \uBCF4\uC774\uC9C0
      \uC54A\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 17
    storyHeading: 
  - id: visual
    english: visual
    meaning: "\uC2DC\uAC01\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Visual signs fail here; I go by smell.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC2DC\uAC01 \uD45C\uC9C0\uB294 \uC5EC\uAE30\uC11C \uC18C\uC6A9\uC5C6\uB2E4.
      \uB0C4\uC0C8\uB85C \uAC04\uB2E4."
    story: 128
    storyHeading: 
  - id: vital
    english: vital
    meaning: "\uD544\uC218\uC801\uC778, \uC0DD\uBA85\uC758"
    partOfSpeech: adj
    example: Salt is vital. So are words, it turns out.
    exampleMeaning: "\uC18C\uAE08\uC740 \uD544\uC218\uB2E4. \uB9D0\uB3C4 \uADF8\uB807\uB2E4\uB294
      \uAC78 \uC54C\uC558\uB2E4."
    story: 74
    storyHeading: "20\uC77C\uC9F8"
  - id: wealth
